SUMMARY:Deadline: ITER 2026: International Touring and Environmental Respon
 sibility Programme
DESCRIPTION:ITER is now in its fifth year and supports people in the perfor
 ming arts to build environmentally responsible\, inclusive and equitable w
 ays of touring and collaborating internationally. It is initiated and fina
 nced by Arts Council England\, Arts Council Norway\, Creative Scotland and
  the Danish Arts Foundation\, and designed and delivered by Julie's Bicycl
 e and in futurum. Creative Scotland joined as a partner for 2026. TWO TRAC
 KS\, up to 60 places in total: PATHFINDERS\, ten sessions introducing the 
 principles of sustainable touring for people new to the subject\; and CHAN
 GEMAKERS\, nine sessions for those further along\, on leadership and syste
 mic change. Seminars run weekly from 1 October to 10 December 2026\, Pathf
 inders on Tuesdays and Changemakers on Thursdays\, 10:00 to 12:00 UK time\
 , alongside networking and a new alumni network. THE MONEY COMES IN PHASE 
 TWO: after the seminars\, groups of three or more participants can apply f
 or up to GBP 30\,000 to implement collaborative projects that cut environm
 ental impacts and advance climate justice. Funding applications open 6 Nov
 ember 2026 and close 11 January 2027\; awarded projects begin in spring 20
 27. ELIGIBILITY: based in England\, Scotland\, Norway or Denmark\, working
  in dance\, music or theatre. That covers music and theatre venues receivi
 ng touring work\, festivals\, orchestras\, theatre companies\, solo artist
 s\, performers and musicians\, performing arts producers\, and multidiscip
 linary arts organisations. Applications are made through the relevant nati
 onal funder (Arts Council England open funds\, Creative Scotland targeted 
 funding\, Arts Council Norway\, Danish Arts Foundation). Questions go to e
 nvironmentaltouring@artscouncil.org.uk.\n\nOrganisation: Julie's Bicycle a
 nd in futurum\, funded by Arts Council England\, Arts Council Norway\, Cre
 ative Scotland and the Danish Arts Foundation\nLocation: England\, Scotlan
 d\, Norway and Denmark. Seminars run online.\nAward: The seminar programme
  is free to join. Groups of three or more participants can then apply for 
 up to GBP 30\,000 for collaborative projects\, with funding applications o
 pen 6 November 2026 to 11 January 2027 and projects starting spring 2027. 
 No application fee.\nDuration: Seminars run weekly from 1 October to 10 De
 cember 2026\, Tuesdays for Pathfinders and Thursdays for Changemakers\, 10
 :00 to 12:00 UK time. Deadline to join 1 September 2026.\n\nMore: https://
